<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/native_or_imported_manual> "native" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/native_term_status> "native_source_seen" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/parentage_quality> "poor" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/preferred_label> "Condition" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/provenance_quality> "good" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/review_date> "2026-07-08" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/reviewer> "MM" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/term> "condition" .
<https://w3id.org/opencare#Condition> <https://example.org/disease-review/ap/upper_level_interpretation> "Strange hierarchy of opencare:Condition is_a ncit:Condition is_a opencare:Clinical Finding is_a sty:Finding is_a bfo:Process. opencare:Clinical finding denotes the result of a clinical examination process but isn't a process. The definition of openare:Condition refers to \"particular state\" which seems like a conflict with opencare:Clinical finding. On the other hand, the definition is rather a description than a logical definition." .
